  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Does the function $f(x)=2x^5+3$ have a stationary point? If it does, determine its nature.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Yes. There is a horizontal point of inflection at $(0,3)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=10x^4\\\\10x^4&=0\\\\x&=0\\\\f(0)&=2(0)^5+3=3\\\\f''(x)&=40x^3\\\\f''(0)&=0\\\\f''(-1)&=-40\\\\f''(1)&=40\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor $x<0$, $f''(x)<0$; for $x>0$, $f''(x)>0$. The concavity changes from down to up at $x=0$. Since $f'(0)=0$, $(0,3)$ is a horizontal point of inflection.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 43
  },
